acant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Hawaii remains one of the most Democratic states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly 13 points toward Democrats and no Republican presidential win in decades. With the incumbent president term-limited, both parties face open nominating contests, and early national primary polling shows frontrunners emerging, but no Hawaii-specific general-election surveys have been conducted.\n\nWithout state polls, expert ratings, or candidate fundraising to sharpen the picture, the forecast rests on the state's fundamental Democratic lean. That points to a Democratic advantage of about 13 points, enough to rank as Likely Democratic rather than Safe, since a strong national Republican year could narrow the gap. Uncertainty is wide, and confidence in the exact margin is moderate until direct polling becomes available.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9253, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8734, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8703, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9295, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.874, "margin": 13.0}]}